  // Medicinal herbs use authored eight-sentence science stories. Traditional
  // uses are described as history and research topics, never as treatment advice.
  cards.push(
    expansionCard("ency-ginger","Ginger","Zingiber officinale",medicinalHerbsAtlas,0,"plant","plant",{kingdom:"Plantae",phylum:"Tracheophyta",className:"Liliopsida",order:"Zingiberales",family:"Zingiberaceae",genus:"Zingiber",species:"Z. officinale",origin:"Tropical Southeast Asia and long cultivated across warm regions",lifespan:"A perennial herb whose underground rhizome can produce new shoots for years",diet:"Makes sugar by photosynthesis and stores energy in an underground rhizome",habitat:"Warm humid gardens with rich, well-drained soil",behavior:"Leafy shoots rise from buds on a branching rhizome beneath the soil",activity:"Grows strongly during warm wet seasons and rests when conditions become cool or dry",fact:"Gingerol and related compounds help create ginger's sharp aroma and flavor.",rarityStars:2,storySentences:["A ginger plant looks leafy above ground, but much of its story is hidden below the soil.","The knobby part called ginger is a rhizome, an underground stem that stores energy and grows new buds.","Gingerol and related plant chemicals give fresh ginger its warm aroma and sharp taste.","People have cooked with ginger and included it in traditional health practices for centuries.","Modern researchers have studied ginger especially for some kinds of nausea, but results depend on the product and situation.","A traditional use is not automatically proof that a treatment works.","Concentrated ginger products may cause side effects or interact with medicines.","The safest science lesson is to observe the plant, ask questions and leave medical use to trusted adults and health professionals."]}),
    expansionCard("ency-turmeric","Turmeric","Curcuma longa",medicinalHerbsAtlas,1,"plant","plant",{kingdom:"Plantae",phylum:"Tracheophyta",className:"Liliopsida",order:"Zingiberales",family:"Zingiberaceae",genus:"Curcuma",species:"C. longa",origin:"South and Southeast Asia",lifespan:"A perennial herb that regrows from underground rhizomes",diet:"Makes sugar by photosynthesis and stores energy in orange rhizomes",habitat:"Warm rainy gardens and farms with fertile, well-drained soil",behavior:"Broad leaves grow from the soil while branching rhizomes enlarge underground",activity:"Grows during warm wet months and may become dormant in a dry season",fact:"Curcumin is one of several pigments that give turmeric its bright yellow-orange color.",rarityStars:3,storySentences:["Turmeric is a leafy tropical herb related to ginger.","Its useful orange part is a rhizome, not a root, because it is an underground stem with buds.","Curcumin and other pigments color the rhizome and can stain food, cloth and fingers yellow.","People have long used turmeric as a spice, dye and part of traditional medical systems.","Scientists study curcumin, but the human body absorbs it poorly and research has not proved every popular claim.","A colorful chemical in a plant can have biological effects without being a cure-all.","Turmeric supplements may cause side effects or interact with medicines.","Children can safely explore its botany and chemistry while leaving treatment decisions to adults and health professionals."]}),
    expansionCard("ency-german-chamomile","German Chamomile","Matricaria chamomilla",medicinalHerbsAtlas,2,"plant","plant",{kingdom:"Plantae",phylum:"Tracheophyta",className:"Magnoliopsida",order:"Asterales",family:"Asteraceae",genus:"Matricaria",species:"M. chamomilla",origin:"Europe and western Asia, now grown widely",lifespan:"An annual herb that flowers, makes seeds and completes its life cycle in one season",diet:"Makes sugar by photosynthesis while roots absorb water and minerals",habitat:"Sunny fields, garden beds and disturbed ground",behavior:"Produces many fragrant flower heads with white rays around a yellow center",activity:"Flowers during warm bright weather and attracts small pollinators",fact:"Each daisy-like head is actually a cluster of many tiny flowers.",rarityStars:2,storySentences:["Chamomile seems to have one little flower, but each flower head is really a crowded community of tiny flowers.","The white ray flowers surround many yellow disk flowers in the center.","Its apple-like scent comes from aromatic chemicals made by the plant.","Dried chamomile flower heads have long been used in drinks and traditional remedies.","Researchers have examined chamomile for several uses, but strong evidence is limited for many claims.","Plants in the daisy family can trigger allergies in some people.","Tea and concentrated extracts are not the same thing and can affect the body differently.","Studying the flower's structure and scent is safe science, while health decisions belong with trusted adults and professionals."]}),
    expansionCard("ency-peppermint","Peppermint","Mentha × piperita",medicinalHerbsAtlas,3,"plant","plant",{kingdom:"Plantae",phylum:"Tracheophyta",className:"Magnoliopsida",order:"Lamiales",family:"Lamiaceae",genus:"Mentha",species:"M. × piperita",origin:"A garden hybrid first recognized in Europe and now cultivated worldwide",lifespan:"A perennial herb that spreads and returns for many years",diet:"Makes sugar by photosynthesis and stores energy in spreading underground stems",habitat:"Moist sunny or partly shaded soil beside gardens and streams",behavior:"Spreads rapidly by runners and produces opposite leaves on square stems",activity:"Grows strongly in cool moist seasons and flowers in summer",fact:"Menthol activates cold-sensing receptors, creating a cool feeling without actually lowering skin temperature.",rarityStars:2,storySentences:["Rub a peppermint leaf gently and the air quickly fills with a cool, sharp scent.","Menthol in the leaf activates some of the same nerve sensors that respond to cold.","That is why peppermint can feel chilly even when its temperature has not changed.","Peppermint is a hybrid plant and usually spreads by underground runners instead of seeds.","People use its leaves for flavor, while researchers have studied specially prepared peppermint oil for a few digestive conditions.","Peppermint candy, tea and concentrated oil contain very different amounts of plant chemicals.","Essential oil can be unsafe if swallowed or used incorrectly, especially around young children.","This plant is a good reminder that a pleasant smell can also be powerful chemistry."]}),
    expansionCard("ency-aloe-vera","Aloe Vera","Aloe vera",medicinalHerbsAtlas,4,"plant","plant",{kingdom:"Plantae",phylum:"Tracheophyta",className:"Liliopsida",order:"Asparagales",family:"Asphodelaceae",genus:"Aloe",species:"A. vera",origin:"Probably the Arabian Peninsula and long cultivated in dry warm regions",lifespan:"A perennial succulent that can live for many years",diet:"Makes sugar by photosynthesis and stores water inside thick leaves",habitat:"Dry sunny ground with fast-draining soil",behavior:"Forms a rosette of water-storing leaves and may produce offsets around the parent plant",activity:"Opens leaf pores mostly at night to reduce water loss",fact:"The clear inner gel and the yellow latex beneath the leaf skin are different substances.",rarityStars:2,storySentences:["Aloe vera survives dry weather by storing water inside thick, fleshy leaves.","It opens many of its leaf pores at night, a water-saving process called CAM photosynthesis.","Inside a cut leaf are clear gel and a yellow latex layer, and they have different chemicals.","People have applied aloe preparations to skin for a long time.","Research suggests some topical aloe products may help certain minor burns, but products and evidence vary.","Swallowing aloe latex can cause serious digestive effects and is not a safe experiment.","Natural does not mean harmless, and different parts of one plant can act differently.","The best classroom activity is to study aloe's water-saving adaptations rather than use it as medicine."]}),
    expansionCard("ency-echinacea","Purple Coneflower","Echinacea purpurea",medicinalHerbsAtlas,5,"plant","plant",{kingdom:"Plantae",phylum:"Tracheophyta",className:"Magnoliopsida",order:"Asterales",family:"Asteraceae",genus:"Echinacea",species:"E. purpurea",origin:"Central and eastern North American prairies and open woods",lifespan:"A perennial herb that returns from its root crown for several years",diet:"Makes sugar by photosynthesis while roots absorb water and minerals",habitat:"Sunny prairie, meadow and garden soil",behavior:"Raises cone-shaped flower heads that provide nectar and seeds for wildlife",activity:"Grows in spring and summer, flowers in warm months and rests in winter",fact:"Its genus name comes from a Greek word for hedgehog, describing the spiny-looking flower center.",rarityStars:3,storySentences:["Purple coneflower lifts a spiny orange cone above a ring of pink-purple petals.","The cone is made of many tiny flowers that later produce seeds for birds.","Its name Echinacea comes from a Greek word connected with hedgehogs.","Indigenous peoples of North America used related Echinacea plants in traditional practices.","Today Echinacea products are often advertised for colds, but studies have produced mixed results.","Different species, plant parts and preparations do not contain exactly the same chemicals.","People allergic to daisy-family plants may also react to Echinacea.","Its pollinators, seeds and prairie adaptations provide reliable science even when medical questions remain uncertain."]}),
    expansionCard("ency-calendula","Calendula","Calendula officinalis",medicinalHerbsAtlas,6,"plant","plant",{kingdom:"Plantae",phylum:"Tracheophyta",className:"Magnoliopsida",order:"Asterales",family:"Asteraceae",genus:"Calendula",species:"C. officinalis",origin:"Probably the Mediterranean region and long cultivated in gardens",lifespan:"Usually an annual or short-lived perennial herb",diet:"Makes sugar by photosynthesis while roots absorb water and minerals",habitat:"Sunny garden beds and well-drained disturbed ground",behavior:"Produces orange flower heads repeatedly and attracts bees and other pollinators",activity:"Flowers through cool to warm seasons when light and water are available",fact:"Calendula is called pot marigold but is not the same genus as common garden marigolds.",rarityStars:2,storySentences:["Calendula opens bright orange flower heads like small suns in a garden.","Each head contains many individual flowers rather than one single flower.","The plant is often called pot marigold, but it is not a true Tagetes marigold.","Its petals have been used as color, food decoration and in traditional skin preparations.","Scientists study plant compounds such as flavonoids and triterpenes, but a laboratory result is not automatically a medical treatment.","Calendula can cause an allergic reaction in people sensitive to the daisy family.","Pollinators may visit the flowers many times while seeds form in curved shapes below.","Its best science story connects naming, pollination, chemistry and careful evaluation of health claims."]}),
    expansionCard("ency-lemongrass","Lemongrass","Cymbopogon citratus",medicinalHerbsAtlas,7,"plant","plant",{kingdom:"Plantae",phylum:"Tracheophyta",className:"Liliopsida",order:"Poales",family:"Poaceae",genus:"Cymbopogon",species:"C. citratus",origin:"Tropical Asia and widely cultivated in warm regions",lifespan:"A perennial grass that regrows from its dense base",diet:"Makes sugar by photosynthesis and absorbs water and minerals through fibrous roots",habitat:"Warm sunny gardens and farms with moist, well-drained soil",behavior:"Forms a thick clump of long narrow leaves with aromatic oil glands",activity:"Grows throughout warm rainy periods and slows in cool weather",fact:"Citral is a major component of the oil that produces the familiar lemon scent.",rarityStars:2,storySentences:["Lemongrass is a true grass even though its leaves smell strongly of lemons.","Tiny oil glands store aromatic chemicals, especially citral.","Citral molecules reach scent receptors in the nose and help the brain recognize a lemon-like smell.","People use lemongrass in cooking, drinks, fragrances and traditional practices.","Researchers study its oils, but concentrated oil is much stronger than a leaf used for flavor.","Essential oils can irritate skin or be unsafe when swallowed incorrectly.","The plant's dense fibrous roots also help hold soil around the clump.","Lemongrass turns an everyday kitchen scent into a lesson about grasses, molecules and safe amounts."]}),
    expansionCard("ency-lemon-balm","Lemon Balm","Melissa officinalis",medicinalHerbsAtlas,8,"plant","plant",{kingdom:"Plantae",phylum:"Tracheophyta",className:"Magnoliopsida",order:"Lamiales",family:"Lamiaceae",genus:"Melissa",species:"M. officinalis",origin:"Europe, North Africa and western Asia",lifespan:"A perennial herb that returns and spreads for several years",diet:"Makes sugar by photosynthesis and stores energy in roots and short underground stems",habitat:"Moist sunny or partly shaded gardens and woodland edges",behavior:"Produces wrinkled lemon-scented leaves on square stems and small flowers rich in nectar",activity:"Grows in spring and summer and may die back during cold winters",fact:"Melissa means honeybee in Greek because the nectar-rich flowers attract bees.",rarityStars:2,storySentences:["Lemon balm has no lemons, yet a brushed leaf releases a citrus-like scent.","It belongs to the mint family and has the family's typical square stems and opposite leaves.","Its scientific name Melissa means honeybee in Greek.","Bees visit the small pale flowers for nectar while helping move pollen.","People have used lemon balm in foods and traditional calming preparations.","Researchers are still examining possible effects, and evidence depends on the preparation and question.","Concentrated products can cause side effects or interact with medicines.","The plant offers a richer story when we connect scent chemistry, stem shape, pollination and careful research."]}),
    expansionCard("ency-holy-basil","Holy Basil","Ocimum tenuiflorum",medicinalHerbsAtlas,9,"plant","plant",{kingdom:"Plantae",phylum:"Tracheophyta",className:"Magnoliopsida",order:"Lamiales",family:"Lamiaceae",genus:"Ocimum",species:"O. tenuiflorum",origin:"The Indian subcontinent and tropical Southeast Asia",lifespan:"A short-lived perennial in warm climates and often grown as an annual elsewhere",diet:"Makes sugar by photosynthesis while roots absorb water and minerals",habitat:"Warm sunny gardens with fertile, well-drained soil",behavior:"Branches into an aromatic bush with small flowers visited by insects",activity:"Grows and flowers during warm bright weather",fact:"Eugenol and other aromatic compounds contribute to the spicy clove-like scent of some varieties.",rarityStars:3,storySentences:["Holy basil, also called tulsi, is an aromatic member of the mint family.","Its square stems carry opposite leaves and upright spikes of many tiny flowers.","Eugenol and other compounds help produce a spicy, clove-like scent.","The plant has cultural and religious importance in South Asia as well as a history of traditional use.","Scientists are studying holy basil, but there is not enough strong evidence to treat it as a cure for many advertised conditions.","A plant can be culturally important even while medical claims still require careful testing.","Extracts may affect the body differently from leaves used in food.","Learning both the biology and the cultural history gives a fuller and more respectful science story."]})
  );

